{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וַיְהִי מִקֵּץ</b> — the reason for its using an expression of distress is that now the cause of the Egyptian exile begins to unfold. And even though the exile had already been decreed by decrees from ancient times, our Rabbis have said that it was not decreed that it would be in Egypt, which is the iron furnace; as the Tosafot wrote in tractate Shabbat (10a), and as the Rabad wrote (Hilkhot Teshuvah, chapter 6), that the Egyptians added suffering beyond what the verse stated: ״וַעֲבָדוּם וְעִנּוּ״ etc. (Genesis 15:13)." "<b>Further,</b> it uses an expression of distress on account of the tidings of famine, for the Holy One, blessed be He, is, as it were, in distress along with the distress of His world. Go out and learn from what they of blessed memory expounded (Megillah 10b) on the verse (Exodus 14:20): ״וְלֹא קָרַב זֶה אֶל זֶה״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to the distress of that righteous man who was held back until two full years had elapsed after the dream of the chief cupbearer, for then he should have gone free; as they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 89:2), and this is their language: ״Because he said, ״כִּי אִם זְכַרְתַּנִי וְהִזְכַּרְתַּנִי״ — two years were added to him.״ You see that two years were added to him in distress. And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 89:2): ״וַיְהִי מִקֵּץ״ — ״He set an end to the darkness.״ According to this, the verse intends along this path: ״וַיְהִי״ — because of ״קֵץ,״ which is the evil inclination, called ״קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר,״ and it is the cause of the distress of the righteous man for two years. And through this we have also accounted for the beginning of this reckoning of two years, which relates to the dream of the chief cupbearer, which was the time of remembering Joseph for good. And the reason for two years is because he said ״זְכַרְתַּנִי וְהִזְכַּרְתַּנִי״ — corresponding to each act of remembering, HaShem withheld his being remembered at the start of the year, which is the time of remembrance." "<b>Further,</b> in saying <b>מִקֵּץ שְׁנָתַיִם</b> it alludes to saying that it was at the very time itself that the chief cupbearer and the chief baker dreamed — which is the time when a dream declares a true thing and does not speak emptiness." "<b>וּפַרְעֹה חוֹלֵם</b> — now, since this is the beginning of the revelation, it should have said ״חָלַם פַּרְעֹה״ or ״וַיַּחֲלוֹם פַּרְעֹה.״ However, the verse refers back to the first matter it related in the preceding portion — the dream of the chief cupbearer, regarding its matter — and therefore adds to say: and Pharaoh too is dreaming. And the intention in this is that if the chief cupbearer did not wish to remember him on account of his dream, HaShem brought it about that Pharaoh too would dream, and through this he was compelled against his will to remember him. And the inferential reading of the verse is along this path: ״וְלֹא זָכַר שַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים וַיְהִי מִקֵּץ״ etc. — ״וּפַרְעֹה גַּם הוּא חוֹלֵם״ etc. — ״וַיְדַבֵּר שַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים בְּעַל כָּרְחוֹ לֵאמֹר״ etc. — ״וְשָׁם אִתָּנוּ״ etc. — ״וַיִּפְתָּר לָנוּ״ etc." "<b>Further</b>, it is possible that it intends to say that for two years' days Pharaoh was continuously dreaming this dream, except that he did not know he had dreamed, and at the end of two years he dreamed and did not forget." "It further intends, in saying <b>and Pharaoh was dreaming</b>, to say that Pharaoh himself perceived in his dream that he was dreaming a dream. Because he saw wondrous things, as I shall explain, he sensed that these are not from things that exist in the world and that this is nothing but a dream. And the verse's intention in this notification is to tell the signs of a dream that will be validated, namely when a person senses in a dream that his mind is sound enough to distinguish things, to the point that he grasps and knows things that could not possibly exist in waking — and he senses that he is dreaming. This indicates that his dream is not a confusion of the mind and a mingling of the intellect with winds tormenting him, but rather supernal beings on high informing him of future events."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:43.836114676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}